<TABLE CELLSPACING=20 CELLPADDING=0 WIDTH=540>

<TR>
<TD VALIGN=TOP COLSPAN=2>
<FONT SIZE=-1>
<FONT COLOR="#D60021">1.</FONT>
<FONT COLOR="#00105A"><A HREF="../eda.htm">Exploratory Data Analysis</a></FONT>
<BR>
<FONT COLOR="#D60021">1.3.</FONT>
<FONT COLOR="#00105A"><A HREF="eda3.htm">EDA Techniques</a></FONT>
<BR>
<FONT COLOR="#D60021">1.3.5.</FONT>
<FONT COLOR="#00105A"><A HREF="eda35.htm">Quantitative Techniques</a></FONT>
<BR>
<FONT COLOR="#D60021">1.3.5.18.</FONT>
<FONT COLOR="#00105A"><A HREF="eda35i.htm">Yates Analysis</a></FONT>
<BR>
</FONT>
<BR>
<TABLE>
<TR>
<TD VALIGN=top>
<H2><FONT COLOR="#D60021">1.3.5.18.2.</FONT></H2>
</TD>
<TD VALIGN=top>
<H2>Important Factors</H2>
</TD>
</TR >
</TABLE>
</TD>
</TR>






<!-- begin paragraph -->
<TR>
<TD WIDTH=15% VALIGN=top>
<!-- Add marginal notes below -->
<I>
Identify Important Factors
</I>
</TD>
<TD WIDTH=85%>
<!-- Add main text below -->
The Yates analysis generates a large number of potential
models.  From this list, we want to select the most appropriate model.
 This requires balancing the following two goals.
<ol>
   <li>We want the model to include all important factors.
   <li>We want the model to be parsimonious.  That is, the model
       should be as simple as possible.
</ol>
In short, we want our model to include all the important factors
and interactions and to omit the unimportant factors and
interactions.
<p>
Seven criteria are utilized to define important factors.  These seven
criteria are not all equally important, nor will they yield identical
subsets, in which case a consensus subset or a weighted consensus
subset must be extracted.
<p>
These criterion will be examined in the context of the
<a href="../../pri/section6/pri611.htm">Eddy current</a>
data set.  The <a href="eda35i.htm#yatestable">Yates Analysis</a>
page gave the sample Yates output for this data and the
<a href="eda35i1.htm#models">Defining Models and Predictions</a>
page listed the potential models from the Yates analysis.

</TD>
</TR>
<!-- end paragraph -->

<!-- begin paragraph -->
<TR>
<TD WIDTH=15% VALIGN=top>
<!-- Add marginal notes below -->
<A NAME="selectioncriterion">
<I>
Criterion for Including Terms in the Model
</I>
</A>
</TD>
<TD WIDTH=85% VALIGN=top>
<!-- Add main text below -->
The seven criterion that we can use in determining whether to
keep a factor in the model can be summarized as follows.
<OL>
   <li><a href="eda35i2.htm#engsig">Effects: Engineering
       Significance</a>
   <li><a href="eda35i2.htm#numsig">Effects: 90% Numeric
       Significance</a>
   <li><a href="eda35i2.htm#statsig">Effects: Statistical
       Significance</a>
   <li><a href="eda35i2.htm#probplot">Effects: Probability Plots</a>
   <li><a href="eda35i2.htm#youden">Averages: Youden Plot</a>
   <li><a href="eda35i2.htm#ressd1">Residual Standard Deviation:
       Engineering Significance</a>
   <li><a href="eda35i2.htm#ressd2">Residual Standard Deviation:
       Statistical Significance</a>
</OL>
The first four criterion focus on effect estimates with 
three numeric criterion and one graphical criterion.
The fifth criterion focuses on averages.  The last two criterion
focus on the residual standard deviation of the model.  We
discuss each of these seven criteria in detail in the
following sections.
<P>
The last section summarizes the
<a href="pri618.htm#conclusions">conclusions</a> based on all of
the criterion.

</TD>
</TR>
<!-- end paragraph -->

<!-- begin paragraph -->
<TR>
<TD WIDTH=15% VALIGN=top>
<!-- Add marginal notes below -->
<A NAME="engsig">
<I>
Effects: Engineering Significance
</I>
</A>
</TD>
<TD WIDTH=85%>
<!-- Add main text below -->
The minimum engineering significant difference is defined as
<UL>
<img align=absmiddle src="eqns/absbetad.gif"
     alt="|betahati|> delta">
</UL>
where
<img align=absmiddle src="eqns/absbeta.gif"
     alt="absolute value of betahat">
is the absolute value of the parameter estimate (i.e., the
effect) and
<img align=absmiddle src="eqns/delta.gif"
     alt="delta">
is the minimum engineering significant difference.
<P>
That is, declare a factor as "important" if the effect is greater
than some a priori declared engineering difference.  This
implies that the engineering has in fact stated as to what a
minimum effect will be.  Oftentimes this is not the case.  In
the absence of an a priori difference, a good rough rule for
the minimum engineering significant
<img align=absmiddle src="eqns/delta.gif"
     alt="delta">
is to keep only those factors whose effect is greater than, say,
10% of the current production average.  In this case, let's say
that the average detector has a sensitivity of 2.5 ohms, then this
would suggest that we would declare all factors whose effect is
greater than 10% of 2.5 ohms = 0.25 ohm to be significant
(from an engineering point of view).
<p>
Based on this minimum engineering significant difference criterion,
we thus conclude to keep two terms: X1 (3.10250) and X2 (-.86750).

</TD>
</TR>
<!-- end paragraph -->

<!-- begin paragraph -->
<TR>
<TD WIDTH=15% VALIGN=top>
<!-- Add marginal notes below -->
<A NAME="numsig">
<I>
Effects: 90% Numeric Significance
</I>
</A>
</TD>
<TD WIDTH=85%>
<!-- Add main text below -->
The 90% numeric significance criterion is defined as
<UL>
<img align=absmiddle src="eqns/absbeta2.gif"
   alt="absolute value of effect i > maximum absolute value effect">
</UL>
That is, declare a factor as "important" if one determines the
largest effect and keep all factors in excess of 10% of that
effect.  We thus are keeping the "top 90%" of all effects.  This
criterion is neither engineering nor statistical, but it does offer
some additional numeric insight.  For the current case study, the
largest effect is from factor 1 (3.10250 ohms), and so 10% of that
is 0.31 ohms, which suggests keeping all factors whose effects
exceed 0.31 ohms.
<P>
Based on the 90% numerics criterion, we thus conclude to keep
three terms:  X1 (3.10250) and X2 (-.86750) and X2*X3 (.29750).

</TD>
</TR>
<!-- end paragraph -->

<!-- begin paragraph -->
<TR>
<TD WIDTH=15% VALIGN=top>
<!-- Add marginal notes below -->
<A NAME="statsig">
<I>
Effects: Statistical Significance
</I>
</A>
</TD>
<TD WIDTH=85%>
<!-- Add main text below -->
Statistical significance is defined as
<UL>
   <img align=absmiddle src="eqns/absbeta3.gif">
</UL>
That is, declare a factor as "important" if its effect is more
than 2 standard deviations away from 0 (0, by definition, meaning
"no effect").  The difficulty with this is that in order to
invoke this we need the
<a valign=absmiddle src="splett3/eqns/sigma.gif" alt="sigma">
= the standard deviation of an observation.  This is problematic
because
<ol>
   <li>the engineer may not know
       <img valign=absmiddle src="eqns/sigma.gif" alt="sigma">;
   <li>the experiment might not have replication, and so a
       model-free estimate of
       <img valign=absmiddle src="eqns/sigma.gif" alt="sigma">
       is not obtainable;
   <li>obtaining an estimate of
       <img valign=absmiddle src="eqns/sigma.gif" alt="sigma">
       by assuming the sometimes- employed assumption of ignoring
       3-term interactions and higher may be incorrect from an
       engineering point of view.
</ol>
For the current case study:
<ol>
   <li>the engineer did <b>not</b> know
       <img valign=absmiddle src="eqns/sigma.gif" alt="sigma">;
   <li>the design (a 2<sup>3</sup> full factorial) did <b>not</b>
       have replication;
   <li>ignoring 3-term interactions and higher leads to an estimate
       of 
       <img valign=absmiddle src="eqns/sigma.gif" alt="sigma">
      based on omitting only a single term: the X1*X2*X3 interaction.
</ol>
Thus for this current case study, if one assumes that the 3-term
interaction is nil and hence represents a single drawing from a
population centered at zero, then an estimate of the standard
deviation of the effect is simply the effect itself (0.1425).
Two such effect standard deviations is 0.2850.  This rule becomes
to keep all
<img valign=absmiddle src="eqns/absbeta.gif"
   alt="absolute value of betai"> > 0.2850.  This results in
keeping three terms: X1 (3.10250), X2 (-.86750), and X1*X2 (.29750).

</TD>
</TR>
<!-- end paragraph -->

<!-- begin paragraph -->
<TR>
<TD WIDTH=15% VALIGN=top>
<!-- Add marginal notes below -->
<A NAME="probplot">
<I>
Effects: Probability Plots
</I>
</A>
</TD>
<TD WIDTH=85%>
<!-- Add main text below -->
<a href="../section3/probplot.htm">Probability plots</a>
can be used in the following manner.
<ol>
   <li>Normal Probability Plot: Keep a factor as "important"
       if it is displaced off the line-through-zero
       on a normal probability plot of the
       effects.
       <p>
   <li>Halfnormal Probability Plot: Keep a factor as "important"
       if it is displaced off the line-near-zero on a halfnormal
       probability plot of the absolute value of effects.
</ol>
Both of these methods are based on the fact that the least squares
estimates of effects for these 2-level orthogonal designs are
simply the difference of averages and so the central limit theorem
suggests that (if no factor was important) the effects should have
a normal distribution and the absolute value of the effects should
have a halfnormal distribution.
<p>
Since the halfnormal probability plot is only concerned with effect
magnitudes as opposed to signed effects (which are subject to the
vagaries of how the initial factor codings +1 and -1 were assigned),
then the halfnormal probability plot is to be preferred over the
normal probability plot.

</TD>
</TR>
<!-- end paragraph -->

<!-- begin paragraph -->
<TR>
<TD WIDTH=15% VALIGN=top>
<!-- Add marginal notes below -->
<A NAME="normprob">
<I>
Normal Probablity Plot of Effects and Halfnormal Probability
Plot of Effects
</I>
</A>
</TD>
<TD WIDTH=85% VALIGN=TOP>
<!-- Add main text below -->
The following plot shows the normal probability plot of the
effects and the halfnormal probability plot of the absolute value
of the effects.
<P>
<A HREF="../../pri/section6/splett3/gifs/normpr_f.gif">
<img src="../../pri/section6/splett3/gifs/normpr_r.gif"
     HEIGHT=425 WIDTH=550 BORDER=0
     alt="probability plots indicate that model should include main effects for factor 1 and factor 2 with no interaction terms">
</A>
<P>
For the case study at hand, both probability plots clearly show
two factors displaced off the line, and from the third plot (with
factor tags included), we see that those two factors are factor 1
and factor 2. All of the remaining five effects are behaving like
random drawings from a normal distribution centered at zero, and
so are deemed to be statistically insignificant.  In conclusion,
this rule keeps two factors: X1 (3.10250) and X2 (-.86750).

</TD>
</TR>
<!-- end paragraph -->

<!-- begin paragraph -->
<TR>
<TD WIDTH=15% VALIGN=top>
<!-- Add marginal notes below -->
<A NAME="youden">
<I>
Effects: Youden Plot
</I>
</A>
</TD>
<TD WIDTH=85%>
<!-- Add main text below -->
A <a href="../section3/youdplot.htm">Youden plot</a> can
be used in the following way.  Keep a factor as "important" if it'
is displaced away from the central-tendancy "bunch" in a Youden
plot of high and low averages.  By definition, a factor is
important when its average response for the low (-1) setting is
signficantly different from its average response for the high (+1)
setting.  Conversely, if the low and high averages are about the
same, then what difference does it make which setting to use and
so why would such a factor be considered important.  This fact in
combination with the intrinsic benefits of the Youden plot for
comparing pairs of items leads to the technique of generating a
Youden plot of the low and high averages.

</TD>
</TR>
<!-- end paragraph -->

<!-- begin paragraph -->
<TR>
<TD WIDTH=15% VALIGN=top>
<!-- Add marginal notes below -->
<A NAME="youdenplots">
<I>
Youden Plot of Effects
</I>
</A>
</TD>
<TD WIDTH=85%>
<!-- Add main text below -->
The following is the Youden plot of the effects.
<P>
<A HREF="../../pri/section6/splett3/gifs/youden_f.gif">
<img src="../../pri/section6/splett3/gifs/youden_r.gif"
     HEIGHT=425 WIDTH=550 BORDER=0
     alt="Youden plot show factor 1 and factor 2 main effects stand out">
</A>
<P>
For the case study at hand, the Youden plot clearly shows a cluster
of points near the grand average (2.65875) with two displaced
points above (factor 1) and below (factor 2).  Based on the Youden
plot, we thus conclude to keep two factors: X1 (3.10250) and
X2 (-.86750).

</TD>
</TR>
<!-- end paragraph -->

<!-- begin paragraph -->
<TR>
<TD WIDTH=15% VALIGN=top>
<!-- Add marginal notes below -->
<A NAME="ressd1">
<I>
Residual Standard Deviation: Engineering Significance
</I>
</A>
</TD>
<TD WIDTH=85%>
<!-- Add main text below -->
This criterion is defined as
<UL>
    Residual Standard Deviation > Cutoff
</UL>
That is, declare a factor as "important" if the cumulative model
that includes the factor (and all larger factors) has a residual
standard deviation smaller than an a prior engineering-specified
minimum residual standard deviation.  
<p>
This criterion is different from the others in that it is
model focused.  In practice, this criterion states that starting
with the largest effect, we cumulatively keep adding terms to 
the model and monitor how the residual standard deviation for each 
progressively more complicated model gets smaller and smaller.  At
some point, the cumulative model will get complicated enough and
comprehensive enough that the resulting residual standard deviation'
will drop below the pre-specified engineering cutoff for the
residual standard deviation.  At such point, we stop adding terms
and declare all of the model-included terms to be "important" and
everything not in the model as "unimportant".
<p>
This approach implies that the engineer has in fact thought through
as to what a minimum residual standard deviation will be.  In
effect, what the engineer can tolerate for the magnitude of the
typical residual (= difference between the raw data and the
predicted value from the model).  In other words, how good does
the engineer want the prediction equation to be.  Unfortunately,
this engineering specification has not always been formulated
and so this criterion becomes moot.
<p>
In the absence of a prior specified cutoff, a good rough rule for
the minimum engineering residual standard deviation is to keep
adding factors until the residual standard deviation just dips
below, say, 5% of the current production average.  For this case
study, let's say that the average detector has a sensitivity of
2.5 ohms, then this would suggest that we would keep adding
factors to the model until the residual standard deviation first
fell below 5% of 2.5 ohms = 0.125 ohms.
<p>
Based on the minimum residual standard deviation criterion, and
by scanning the far right column of the
<a href="pri615.htm#yatestable">Yates table</a>, we would
conclude to keep the following terms:
<P>
<table cellpadding=0 noborder>
   <tr>
     <td valign=top width=20%>
        <ol start=1>
        <li>X1
        </ol>
     </td>
     <td valign=top width=80%>
          (with a cumulative residual standard deviation = 0.57272)
     </td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
     <td valign=top width=20%>
        <ol start=2>
        <li>X2
        </ol>
     </td>
     <td valign=top width=80%>
        (with a cumulative residual standard deviation = 0.30429)
     </td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
     <td valign=top width=20%>
        <ol start=3>
        <li>X1*X2
        </ol>
     </td>
     <td valign=top width=80%>
        (with a cumulative residual standard deviation = 0.26737) 
     </td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
     <td valign=top width=20%>
        <ol start=4>
        <li>X1*X3
        </ol>
     </td>
     <td valign=top width=80%>
        (with a cumulative residual standard deviation = 0.23341) 
     </td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
     <td valign=top width=20%>
        <ol start=5>
        <li>X3
        </ol>
     </td>
     <td valign=top width=50%>
        (with a cumulative residual standard deviation = 0.19121)
     </td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
     <td valign=top width=20%>
        <ol start=6>
        <li>X1*X2*X3
        </ol>
     </td>
     <td valign=top width=80%>
        (with a cumulative residual standard deviation = 0.18031)
     </td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
     <td valign=top width=20%>
        <ol start=7>
        <li>X1*X2
        </ol>
     </td>
     <td valign=top width=80%>
        (with a cumulative residual standard deviation = 0.00000)
     </td>
   </tr>
</table>
<P>
Note that we must include <b>all</b> terms in order to drive the
residual standard deviation below 0.125.  Again, the 5% is a
rough-and-ready rule which has no basis in engineering or
statistics, but rather "numerics".  Ideally, the engineer has
a better cutoff for the residual standard deviation which is based
on how well he/she wants the equation to peform in practice.  If
such a number were available, then for this criterion and case
study, we would end up with something less than the entire
collection of factors.

</TD>
</TR>
<!-- end paragraph -->

<!-- begin paragraph -->
<TR>
<TD WIDTH=15% VALIGN=top>
<!-- Add marginal notes below -->
<A NAME="ressd2">
<I>
Residual Standard Deviation: Statistical Significance
</I>
</A>
</TD>
<TD WIDTH=85%>
<!-- Add main text below -->
This criterion is defined as
<UL>
    Residual Standard Deviation >
    <img src="eqns/sigma.gif alt="sigma">
</UL>
where <img valign=absmiddle src="eqns/sigma.gif alt="sigma">
is the standard deviation of an observation under replicated
conditions.
<P>
That is, declare a factor as "important" until the cumulative
model that includes the factor (and all larger factors) has a
residual standard deviation smaller than
<img valign=absmiddle src="eqns/sigma.gif alt="sigma">.
In essence, we are allowing that we cannot demand a model
fit any better than what we would get if we had replicated data;
that is, we cannot demand that the residual standard deviation
from any fitted model be any smaller than the (theoretical or
actual) replication standard deviation.  We can drive the fitted
standard deviation down (by adding terms) until it achieves a
value close to
<img valign=absmiddle src="eqns/sigma.gif alt="sigma">,
but to drive it down further means that we are in effect fitting
noise.
<P>
In practice, this criterion may be difficult to apply because
<P>
<OL>
   <LI>the engineer may not know
       <img valign=absmiddle src="eqns/sigma.gif
            alt="sigma">;
   <LI>the experiment might not have replication, and so a
       model-free estimate of
       <img valign=absmiddle src="eqns/sigma.gif
            alt="sigma">
       is not obtainable.
</OL>
<P>
For the current case study:
<P>
<OL>
   <li>the engineer did <b>not</b> know
       <img valign=absmiddle src="eqns/sigma.gif
            alt="sigma">;
   <li>the design (a 2<sup>3</sup> full factorial) did <b>not</b>
       have replication.  The most common way of having replication
       in such designs is to have replicated center points
       at the center of the cube ((X1,X2,X3) = (0,0,0)).  That is,
       at
       <UL>
          number of turns = 135 <br>
          winding distance = 0.76 <br>
          wire guage = 44
       </UL>
</OL>
<P>
Thus for this current case, this criterion could <b>not</b> be
used to yield a subset of "important" factors.

</TD>
</TR>
<!-- end paragraph -->

<!-- begin paragraph -->
<TR>
<TD WIDTH=15% VALIGN=top>
<!-- Add marginal notes below -->
<A NAME="conclusions">
<I>
Conclusions
</I>
</A>
</TD>
<TD WIDTH=85%>
<!-- Add main text below -->
In summary, the seven criterion for specifying "important" factors
yielded the following:
<P>
<table cellpadding=0 noborder>
   <tr>
     <td valign=top width=50%>
        <ol start=1>
        <li>Effects, Engineering Significant:
        </ol>
     </td>
     <td valign=top width=50%>
        X1 X2
     </td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
     <td valign=top width=50%>
        <ol start=2>
        <li>Effects, Numerically Significant:
        </ol>
     </td>
     <td valign=top width=50%>
        X1 X2 X2*X3
     </td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
     <td valign=top width=50%>
        <ol start=3>
        <li>Effects, Statistically Significant:
        </ol>
     </td>
     <td valign=top width=50%>
        X1 X2 X2*X3
     </td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
     <td valign=top width=50%>
        <ol start=4>
        <li>Effects, Probability Plots:
        </ol>
     </td>
     <td valign=top width=50%>
        X1 X2
     </td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
     <td valign=top width=50%>
        <ol start=5>
        <li>Averages, Youden Plot:
        </ol>
     </td>
     <td valign=top width=50%>
        X1 X2
     </td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
     <td valign=top width=50%>
        <ol start=6>
        <li>Residual SD, Engineering Significant:
        </ol>
     </td>
     <td valign=top width=50%>
        all 7 terms
     </td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
     <td valign=top width=50%>
        <ol start=7>
        <li>Residual SD, Statistical Significance:
        </ol>
     </td>
     <td valign=top width=50%>
        not applicable
     </td>
   </tr>
</table>
<P>
Such conflicting results are common.  Arguably, the three most
important criteria (listed in order of most importantance) are:
<P>
<table cellpadding=0 noborder>
   <tr>
     <td valign=top width=50%>
        <ol start=4>
        <li>Effects, Probability Plots:
        </ol>
     </td>
     <td valign=top width=50%>
        X1 X2
     </td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
     <td valign=top width=50%>
        <ol start=1>
        <li>Effects, Engineering Significant:
        </ol>
     </td>
     <td valign=top width=50%>
        X1 X2
     </td>
   </tr>
   <tr>
     <td valign=top width=50%>
        <ol start=3>
        <li>Residual SD, Engineering Significant:
        </ol>
     </td>
     <td valign=top width=50%>
        all 7 terms
     </td>
   </tr>
</table>
<P>
Scanning all of the above, we thus declare the following consensus:
<ol>
   <li>Important Factors: X1 and X2
   <li>Parsimonious Prediction Equation:
       <P>
       <UL>
          <img src="eqns/yhateq3.gif"
               alt="Yhat = 2.65875 + 1/2 (3.10250*X1 - .86750*X2)">
       </UL>
       <P>
       (with a residual standard deviation of .30429 ohms)
</ol>

</TD>
</TR>
<!-- end paragraph -->











</TABLE>

